676 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (      For the first criteria, the robot is required to dispense the correct amount of food into the bowl depending on the bowl colour. To meet this criteria, the code for the trapdoor mechanism \(in green, shown above\) was calibrated to be open for different amounts of time depending on the colour of the bowl. \(purple--the larger bowl, and blue--the smaller bowl\).)
/E (      For the first criteria, the robot is required to dispense the correct amount of food into the bowl depending on the bowl colour. To meet this criteria, the code for the trapdoor mechanism \(in green, shown above\) was calibrated to be open for different amounts of time depending on the colour of the bowl. \(purple--the larger bowl, and blue--the smaller bowl\).)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
677 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (6 )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
678 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      Changes: Previously, this criteria was only to dispense food into one bowl. However, it was then decided that this idea was too simple, so the criteria was changed so that the robot dispenses different amounts of food depending on the bowl colour.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (     For the second criteria, the robot must dispense the food at the correct time. To accomplish this, a lever arm was created using the VEX Lego with a bumper sensor being attached to the end of it. It will extend down so that it is parallel to the floor \(similar to a barrier gate for car parking\), and will stop moving forward and dispense the food when the bumper on the arm touches the bowl.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      Changes: Instead of having a guiding mechanism at the front to align the robot, with the food dispensing on the front, the food will dispense from the side and the lever will prompt the robot to dispense the food at the right time.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
681 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (3.3 Criteria and Constraint implementation)
/E (3.3 Criteria and Constraint implementation)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
682 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      Overall, all of the criteria and constraints have helped in deciding the best design for the robot. From the criteria, the robot needed to dispense food at the correct time and in the correct amount depending on the bowl colour. To do this, the code was changed so that the trapdoor was opened for different amounts of time depending on the hue that the optical sensor detected \(purple or blue\), and the lever arm with the bumper sensor was added to the design to aid in the timing of the dispensing to let the robot know when to stop.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
683 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      From the constraints, the robot needed to be close enough to the bowl for the optical sensor to detect the hue of the bowl properly and self align. So, the lever arm was added. Additionally, the robot was constrained to be small enough to be stored away and the container needed to be easily removable for cleaning. Hence, the lever arm is lifted while the robot moves back home and there was a 3D printed base to hold the container.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
