720 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (      All of the SolidWorks designed parts were 3D printed at the Printing Office in E7 using the Bambu Lab printers and green and black PLA. The printing process showed some struggles as the prints kept failing since it was a very large assembly to print. To combat this, some larger parts of the assembly were split to be printed separately and glued together later. The black container holder was printed separate from the green base which attaches it to the VEX parts and slide. The slide was split from the base with a lip to allow the slide to be glued to the base in the exact orientation it was intended with more surface area for the glue to stick to, giving a stronger fastening point. Printing the slide separate from the base was important to optimize printing time and filament usage, as the slide would have needed a large amount of support material in every potential printing configuration. Our base pieces and slide were printed with split colours because the printer ran out of filament half way through the printing time, and was reloaded with a different colour PLA, however this colour combination adds to the aesthetics of the design. Once the prints finally ran through properly, after about three days worth of attempting to print, the pieces were ready to be assembled with the robot chassis and VEX supports. The container fit perfectly in the designated space in the holder, and since the printed holder base was designed in context to the VEX pieces, all of the holes matched up with the VEX support base, allowing for it to be pieced together using the Lego pins and fasteners. All the separated pieces were glued together and the trapdoor mechanism was able to be tested and refined.)
/E (      All of the SolidWorks designed parts were 3D printed at the Printing Office in E7 using the Bambu Lab printers and green and black PLA. The printing process showed some struggles as the prints kept failing since it was a very large assembly to print. To combat this, some larger parts of the assembly were split to be printed separately and glued together later. The black container holder was printed separate from the green base which attaches it to the VEX parts and slide. The slide was split from the base with a lip to allow the slide to be glued to the base in the exact orientation it was intended with more surface area for the glue to stick to, giving a stronger fastening point. Printing the slide separate from the base was important to optimize printing time and filament usage, as the slide would have needed a large amount of support material in every potential printing configuration. Our base pieces and slide were printed with split colours because the printer ran out of filament half way through the printing time, and was reloaded with a different colour PLA, however this colour combination adds to the aesthetics of the design. Once the prints finally ran through properly, after about three days worth of attempting to print, the pieces were ready to be assembled with the robot chassis and VEX supports. The container fit perfectly in the designated space in the holder, and since the printed holder base was designed in context to the VEX pieces, all of the holes matched up with the VEX support base, allowing for it to be pieced together using the Lego pins and fasteners. All the separated pieces were glued together and the trapdoor mechanism was able to be tested and refined.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
