/E (      In this program, constants store fixed values that are used throughout the program for easy access, if any updates are necessary. Sensors \(inertial, optical and bumper\) store dynamic readings such as orientation, color hue, and pressed or unpressed states. These are read in real-time to control movement and actions. Motors store encoder positions, which is used by the program to move the robot and operate the robot. Lastly, local variables inside functions store constant, dynamic and temporary data. They contain data such as current motor positions, detected colors and food dispensing times. Display information is also stored on the Brain screen. The combination of data storage used, allows the robot to function efficiently and correctly. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
913 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (5.2 Software Design Decisions and Trade-offs)
/E (5.2 Software Design Decisions and Trade-offs)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
914 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      Several software decisions were the result of practical constraints the team encountered while testing. The team initially planned to use the VEX remote for operational control. However, that required the use of callback functions, and it was explicitly stated that callback functions could not be used. Instead, a touch sensor was integrated and programmed as the on/off button for the robot. For colour detection, hue values were used instead of the built-in color reading because the color output was inconsistent and unreliable under different lighting conditions. Another compromise was the method used to re-position the robot after identifying the bowl. Although calculating the encoder counts needed to safely pass the bowl would have been more precise, the team did not have enough time to measure and test the correct encoder values. As a result, a timer was used as a simpler alternative, causing the robot to be less precise. Lastly, the main feeding cycle was designed to run based on real-time sensor detections rather than a fixed number of cycles. This ensured that the robot would continue searching for the correct number of bowls, regardless of the amount of unexpected objects it ran into. This design choice increased the complexity of the control logic, but provided increased reliability and flexibility during the robot demonstration.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
915 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (5.3 Program Testing and Validation)
/E (5.3 Program Testing and Validation)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
916 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      Throughout the development of the software, testing was continuously performed to ensure correct behavior at every stage. Each function was tested individually, before being integrated into the main routine. This ensured that the sensor readings, motor outputs and timing-based actions were working as expected. After the individual tests, full cycle tests were conducted, with various environment changes such as using different bowl arrangements, lighting conditions, and unexpected objects, to ensure the robot behaved consistently in a variety of environments. The testing data is summarized in the table below. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
