964 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (dispense certain portions of food into multiple bowls and return to its starting position using provided VEX components and 3D printed pieces. Throughout the entire design and manufacturing process, changes needed to be made in structural designs and software logic. In the end, the robot was able to fulfill its line of tasks consisting of configuring the sensors and motors, driving beside the food bowls, delivering visibly consistent portions into each bowl, and driving back to its starting position. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
965 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      Although 8/10 of the demonstrations using finalized mechanical structures and software were able to accurately complete the objective, 2 of the trials encountered food getting jammed in the trapdoor, causing flow of food to stop before the desired amount of time and not allowing the trapdoor to be properly closed. This created minor inconsistencies in the portioning of food that was dispensed. This also created issues when the robot began to drive forward after dispensing food. The movement of the robot sometimes dislodged the trapdoor, causing some food to flow out before the robot reached the next bowl. This became extremely inconvenient to clean up and reset. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
966 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      An impracticality of the design was that the bowls had to be lined up and the robot had to be well-positioned beside the bowls in order for the bumper arm to make contact with them. During the testing phase this had proven to be difficult. If the robot was even slightly misaligned with the line-up of bowls, it would veer off into a slight diagonal route, causing the bumper arm to be unable to make contact with any of the bowls. This small variance in the set-up of the demonstration required the robot needing to constantly be reset so that it precisely aligned with the bowls. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
